A junior developer pastes this snippet into the console:
const n = 3100;
console.log(n == '3100');
From this trace, what boolean is printed?

The library helper below is called once:
const n = 3101;
if (n) { console.log('yes'); } else { console.log('no'); }
In this run, which word is printed?

Here is a minimal reproduction of the behavior:
const n = 3102;
console.log(typeof n);
Explain what this code prints and why that value follows from the language rules.

Inspect this program and report the printed value:
const n = 3103;
console.log(Number.isNaN(n / 'x'));
On this input, what does the log show?

Reproduce the behavior of these statements:
const raw = '3104';
console.log(parseInt(raw, 10));
From this trace, what integer is printed?

In a representative debugging session, a student inspects the code. Consider the following JavaScript statements:
let total = 0;
for (const x of [3105, 2, 3]) { total += x; }
console.log(total);
